.TH TREE 1
.SH NAME
Tree \- a window's syntax tree, as an outline, in acme
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B Tree
[
.I winid
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tree
in the tag of an
.IR acme (1)
window opens a window,
.IB file +Tree\f1,
that draws the parse of the first window's text as an outline.
The grammar is the one
.IR hl (1)
would color the file by, from the rules of
.B $home/lib/ts
or
.BR /lib/ts ,
and the parse is its program's answer to a
.B tree
request.
Given
.IR winid ,
it shows that window instead of
.BR $winid .
.PP
Each row is a named node of the parse:
the name of its field in its parent, if it has one, its type, and some of its text:
a definition's name, a leaf's text, or, dimmer, the first line of a larger node's.
A triangle at the left of a node with children points down at an open node and right at a closed one.
At first the definitions that the language's
.B tags.scm
finds are open, with the nodes that hold them,
and the rest closed;
for a language without
.BR tags.scm ,
every node starts open.
.PP
.I Tree
follows the window, as
.I hl
does, by its
.B rdevent
file
.RI ( acme (4)):
after an edit it asks for the parse again, and the nodes still there stay open or closed as they were.
It highlights the row of the node at the window's selection,
or of the nearest open node that holds it.
.PP
Button 1 on a triangle opens or closes its node.
Button 1 on a row selects its node and sets the window's selection to the node's text, and shows it.
The wheel scrolls.
.PP
.BI | cmd
in
.IR Tree 's
tag runs
.I cmd
with
.IR rc (1)
in the file's directory, the selected node's text on its standard input,
and puts what it prints in the node's place in the window,
unless the command fails or the text changed while it ran.
When the node's text has no newline at its end,
.I Tree
drops one the command adds there.
.PP
.IR Tree 's
own text is the outline, a row to a line, indented by tabs, with
.L ▸
and
.L ▾
for the triangles; its selection is the selected row, for programs that read it.
Its colors are
.IR theme (6)'s
.B body
and
.BR bodysel ,
with
.B comment
for field names and the dim text, and
.B function
for definitions' names.
.B Del
ends it, and so does deleting the window it shows.
.SH EXAMPLES
In a C file's window, run
.BR Tree ,
select a function's row with button 1, then type this in its tag and sweep it with button 2
.IP
.EX
|sed 's/count/n/g'
.EE
.PP
to rename a variable in that function alone.
.SH FILES
.TF /sys/lib/ts/lang/tags.scm
.TP
.B /lib/ts
the languages' rules
.TP
.BI /sys/lib/ts/ lang /tags.scm
what a language's definitions are
.TP
.BI /bin/ts/ lang
a language's program
.PD
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/Tree.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR acme (1),
.IR hl (1),
.IR acme (4),
.IR theme (6)
.SH BUGS
Each edit sends the whole text to the grammar's program and reads back every node,
so a long file is slow to follow.
